/-
# Subformations of the formation generated by a group (Kourovka Notebook Problem 16.31)

Kourovka Notebook, Archive of Solved Problems, Problem 16.31 (V. A. Vedernikov): suppose that a
group `G` has a composition series and let `𝔉(G)` be the formation generated by `G`. Is the set of
all subformations of `𝔉(G)` finite?
-/

module

public import Batteries.Util.ProofWanted
public import Mathlib.GroupTheory.Index

@[expose] public section

namespace MathlibExt.GroupTheory.FormationSubformationsFiniteKourovka16Problem31Wanted

/-!
Source: E. I. Khukhro and V. D. Mazurov (eds.), Unsolved Problems in Group Theory: The Kourovka
Notebook, 21st issue, arXiv:1401.0300v49 (2026), Archive of Solved Problems, Problem 16.31 (V. A.
Vedernikov). UnsolvedMath record UM-00701223 (`KOU-16.31`), dataset ulamai/UnsolvedMath v1.8.0.

Stated for finite `G`, where the Archive's counterexample lives, so the refutation is the stronger
statement. Formations are classes of finite groups in `Type` closed under homomorphic images and
finite subdirect products (`G / N₁, G / N₂ ∈ 𝔉` implies `G / (N₁ ∩ N₂) ∈ 𝔉`). A class is a predicate
on finite groups; closure under homomorphic images makes it closed under isomorphism, so distinct
formations are distinct predicates. The formation generated by `G` is the intersection of all
formations containing `G`.
-/

/-- **Classes of finite groups.** A class of finite groups is a predicate on finite groups: it
assigns to every finite group $G$ a proposition, read as "$G$ belongs to the class". The class need
not be closed under isomorphism. -/
abbrev FiniteGroupClass : Type 1 :=
  ∀ (G : Type) [Group G] [Finite G], Prop

/-- **Formations of finite groups, possibly empty.** A class $\mathfrak{F}$ of finite groups is a
formation if it is closed under homomorphic images (whenever $f \colon G \to H$ is a surjective
homomorphism of finite groups and $G \in \mathfrak{F}$, then $H \in \mathfrak{F}$) and under finite
subdirect products (whenever $N_1$ and $N_2$ are normal subgroups of a finite group $G$ with
$G / N_1 \in \mathfrak{F}$ and $G / N_2 \in \mathfrak{F}$, then
$G / (N_1 \cap N_2) \in \mathfrak{F}$). In particular the empty class is a formation. -/
def IsFormation (F : FiniteGroupClass) : Prop :=
  (∀ (G H : Type) [Group G] [Finite G] [Group H] [Finite H] (f : G →* H),
      Function.Surjective f → F G → F H) ∧
    ∀ (G : Type) [Group G] [Finite G] (N₁ N₂ : Subgroup G) [N₁.Normal] [N₂.Normal],
      F (G ⧸ N₁) → F (G ⧸ N₂) → F (G ⧸ (N₁ ⊓ N₂))

/-- **Formation generated by a finite group.** For a finite group $G$, the formation generated by
$G$ is the class of finite groups $H$ such that $H$ belongs to every formation that contains $G$;
that is, it is the intersection of all formations containing $G$. -/
def formationGeneratedBy (G : Type) [Group G] [Finite G] : FiniteGroupClass :=
  fun (H : Type) [Group H] [Finite H] => ∀ F : FiniteGroupClass, IsFormation F → F G → F H

/-- **Kourovka Problem 16.31 for finite groups.** The statement (V. A. Vedernikov) that for every
finite group $G$ only finitely many formations of finite groups are contained in the formation
generated by $G$. Here formations may be empty, and a formation $\mathfrak{F}$ is contained in the
formation generated by $G$ when every finite group in $\mathfrak{F}$ belongs to the formation
generated by $G$. -/
def finitelyManySubformations : Prop :=
  ∀ (G : Type) [Group G] [Finite G],
    {F : FiniteGroupClass | IsFormation F ∧
      ∀ (H : Type) [Group H] [Finite H], F H → formationGeneratedBy G H}.Finite

/--
**The formation generated by a finite group can have infinitely many subformations.** There is
a finite group $G$ such that infinitely many formations are contained in the formation generated
by $G$. That is, the answer to Kourovka Problem 16.31 is negative, even for finite $G$.

Source: E. I. Khukhro and V. D. Mazurov (eds.), Unsolved Problems in Group Theory: The Kourovka
Notebook, 21st issue, arXiv:1401.0300v49 (2026), Archive of Solved Problems, Problem 16.31 (V. A.
Vedernikov); resolution: V. P. Burichenko, J. Algebra 372 (2012), 428-458, Kourovka Archive, Problem
16.31 (No, not always, even for G finite).
-/
public theorem_wanted finitelyManySubformations_refuted : ¬ finitelyManySubformations

end MathlibExt.GroupTheory.FormationSubformationsFiniteKourovka16Problem31Wanted
